{"id":"storage-projected-volumes","corpus":"k8s","title":"Projected Volumes","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/projected-volumes/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Projected Volumes"},{"kind":"p","text":"This document describes projected volumes in Kubernetes. Familiarity with volumes is suggested."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Introduction"},{"kind":"p","text":"A projected volume maps several existing volume sources into the same directory."},{"kind":"p","text":"Currently, the following types of volume sources can be projected:"},{"kind":"li","text":"secret"},{"kind":"li","text":"downwardAPI"},{"kind":"li","text":"configMap"},{"kind":"li","text":"serviceAccountToken"},{"kind":"li","text":"clusterTrustBundle"},{"kind":"li","text":"podCertificate"},{"kind":"p","text":"All sources are required to be in the same namespace as the Pod. For more details, see the all-in-one volume design document."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Example configuration: secrets with a non-default permission mode set"},{"kind":"p","text":"Each projected volume source is listed in the spec under sources. The parameters are nearly the same with two exceptions:"},{"kind":"li","text":"For secrets, the secretName field has been changed to name to be consistent with ConfigMap naming."},{"kind":"li","text":"The defaultMode can only be specified at the projected level and not for each volume source. However, as illustrated above, you can explicitly set the mode for each individual projection."},{"kind":"h2","text":"serviceAccountToken projected volumes"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can inject the token for the current service account into a Pod at a specified path. For example:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The example Pod has a projected volume containing the injected service account token. Containers in this Pod can use that token to access the Kubernetes API server, authenticating with the identity of the pod's ServiceAccount. The audience field contains the intended audience of the token. A recipient of the token must identify itself with an identifier specified in the audience of the token, and otherwise should reject the token. This field is optional and it defaults to the identifier of the API server."},{"kind":"p","text":"The expirationSeconds is the expected duration of validity of the service account token. It defaults to 1 hour and must be at least 10 minutes (600 seconds). An administrator can also limit its maximum value by specifying the --service-account-max-token-expiration option for the API server. The path field specifies a relative path to the mount point of the projected volume."},{"kind":"p","text":"A container using a projected volume source as a subPath volume mount will not receive updates for those volume sources."},{"kind":"h2","text":"clusterTrustBundle projected volumes"},{"kind":"p","text":"More information about this feature"},{"kind":"p","text":"This is a stable feature in Kubernetes, and has been since version 1.37. It was first available in the v1.29 release."},{"kind":"p","text":"The clusterTrustBundle projected volume source injects the contents of one or more ClusterTrustBundle objects as an automatically-updating file in the container filesystem."},{"kind":"p","text":"ClusterTrustBundles can be selected either by name or by signer name."},{"kind":"p","text":"To select by name, use the name field to designate a single ClusterTrustBundle object."},{"kind":"p","text":"To select by signer name, use the signerName field (and optionally the labelSelector field) to designate a set of ClusterTrustBundle objects that use the given signer name. If labelSelector is not present, then all ClusterTrustBundles for that signer are selected."},{"kind":"p","text":"The kubelet deduplicates the certificates in the selected ClusterTrustBundle objects, normalizes the PEM representations (discarding comments and headers), reorders the certificates, and writes them into the file named by path. As the set of selected ClusterTrustBundles or their content changes, kubelet keeps the file up-to-date."},{"kind":"p","text":"By default, the kubelet will prevent the pod from starting if the named ClusterTrustBundle is not found, or if signerName / labelSelector do not match any ClusterTrustBundles. If this behavior is not what you want, then set the optional field to true, and the pod will start up with an empty file at path."},{"kind":"h2","text":"podCertificate projected volumes"},{"kind":"p","text":"More information about this feature"},{"kind":"p","text":"This is a stable feature in Kubernetes, and has been since version 1.37. It was first available in the v1.34 release."},{"kind":"p","text":"The podCertificate projected volumes source securely provisions a private key and X.509 certificate chain for pod to use as client or server credentials. Kubelet will then handle refreshing the private key and certificate chain when they get close to expiration. The application just has to make sure that it reloads the file promptly when it changes, with a mechanism like inotify or polling."},{"kind":"p","text":"Each podCertificate projection supports the following configuration fields:"},{"kind":"li","text":"signerName: The signer you want to issue the certificate. Note that signers may have their own access requirements, and may refuse to issue certificates to your pod."},{"kind":"li","text":"keyType: The type of private key that should be generated. Valid values are ED25519, ECDSAP256, ECDSAP384, ECDSAP521, RSA3072, and RSA4096."}],"lemmas":{"projected":["projected","project"],"projecteds":["projecteds","projected"],"projects":["project","projects"],"projecting":["projecting","project"],"volumes":["volume","volumes"],"documents":["document","documents"],"documenting":["documenting","document"],"documented":["documented","document"],"describes":["describes","describe"],"described":["describ